<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Without this the app is unsigned, has no entitlements at all, and every
         keychain write fails with errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018). The device
         then cannot hold an identity: each call generated a key, failed to store
         it, and generated another next time — so it authenticated with one key
         and displayed a different one to authorize, which looks exactly like a
         host rejecting a correct key.

         `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` is not decoration. A real provisioning profile
         grants `TEAMID.*` and nothing else, so a bare group name matches
         nothing the profile allows and the device build fails to sign at all.
         The prefix expands to empty when there is no profile, which is what
         keeps the ad-hoc simulator build signing exactly as before.

         `$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)` rather than a literal, so the group is
         this channel's alone. It was `com.farcooler.ios` for every build, which
         made the keychain the one thing four channels shared: canary and stable
         installed side by side could read each other's device identity, and a
         device key is precisely what the isolation everywhere else — runtime
         directory, database, WorkOS environment, APNs topic, bundle id — exists
         to keep apart.

         Following PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER rather than naming the channels
         means there is no second list to keep in step with generate-project.py,
         and it is why this migrates nothing: for stable that setting IS
         `com.farcooler.ios`, so the group it expands to is byte-for-byte the
         one every existing install already writes to. Only the three channels
         that have never shipped move. -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Remote notifications. `development` so a locally-signed build gets a
         sandbox APNs token; the App Store build is re-signed with `production`
         automatically. Without this key the app gets no device token at all and
         the daemon can never reach a sleeping phone — which is the case the
         whole notification path exists for. -->
    <key>aps-environment</key>
    <string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
